<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Academy of Gundishapur</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Academy of Gundishapur was a centre of learning in the city of Gundishapur in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a>. It was especially noted for medicine and brought together Greek, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a>, Persian and Indian knowledge. Its traditions of scholarship later influenced intellectual life in the early Islamic world.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/academy-of-gundishapur.jpg" alt="Academy of Gundishapur" width="320" height="239" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alireza.heydear · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ancient_City_of_Gandhi_Shapour_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Academy of Gondishapur, also called Gondishapur University, was a Sassanid-era centre of education in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khuzestan">Khuzestan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, founded by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shapur-i">Shapur I</a>. It first offered education and training in medicine, philosophy, theology and science, and it also included a hospital and a library. According to Richard Frye, the name is a corrupted form of Veh-Andiukh-Shapur, meaning better than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>, built by Shapur.</p><p>Ibn al-Nadim writes that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardashir-i">Ardashir I</a> collected scattered ancient Persian books from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> for the royal treasury and that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shapur-i">Shapur I</a> continued this, gathering texts translated into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-persian">Middle Persian</a> and compiling the Avesta into a single book. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-i">Khosrow I</a> later built a library for the university at Gundishapur. The available historical information is insufficient for a precise picture, and Islamic-era accounts of the scholarly past often border on legend.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 489 the Byzantine emperor Zeno ordered the East Syriac Christian school at Edessa closed, and it was absorbed into the School of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nisibis">Nisibis</a>, then under Persian rule. There Nestorian scholars and Hellenistic philosophers banished from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> by Justinian in 529 worked on medicine, astronomy and mathematics. Under Khosrau I (531-579) Gondishapur became known for medicine and learning. He gave refuge to Greek philosophers and to Syriac-speaking Nestorian Christians fleeing Byzantine persecution, and had them translate Greek and Syriac texts on medicine, astronomy, philosophy and useful crafts into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-persian">Middle Persian</a>.</p><p>He also sent the physician Borzouye to invite Indian and Chinese scholars, who translated Indian texts on astronomy, mathematics and medicine and Chinese texts on herbal medicine and religion. Almost all the physicians were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a>, but they wrote in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a>, because medicine had a literary tradition in that language.</p><p>In 832 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mamun">al-Ma'mun</a> strengthened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wisdom">House of Wisdom</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, which was staffed with graduates of Gondishapur and copied its methods. The intellectual centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a> had moved to Baghdad, and references to Gondishapur became few. The last known head of its hospital died in 869, and al-Muqaddasi, about 1000, described it as falling into ruins.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The hospital of Gondishapur combined treatment with medical training, and according to most sources on the history of medicine it transmitted Greek, Iranian and Indian medical knowledge into the Islamic world, whose first hospitals were modelled on it. Students worked in the hospital under the supervision of the whole medical faculty rather than with one physician, and graduates may have had to pass examinations. Notable physicians include Borzuya, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhtishu">Bukhtishu</a>, Masawaiyh and Sabur ibn Sahl. In 1955 Jondishapur University and its twin, a medical university, were founded near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahvaz">Ahvaz</a> in its memory, and the ancient site is now an archaeological site.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Academy of Gondishapur” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The king welcomed Neoplatonist philosophers who left Athens after Justinian's closure of the Neoplatonic Academy in 529 CE, sponsored translations from Greek and Sanskrit, and encouraged medical learning at <b>Gundishapur</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
